re talking with them. For example, ‘What do you do, Hiro?’ or, ‘Mr. Sakamoto, where is your office?’ Using names has two purposes: First, it shows respect to the person you are talking to. Second, it makes it clear that the question is directed to someone specific, so there can be no misunderstanding. When we first meet someone, it also has the benefit of confirming our counterpart</ns0:t></ns0:r><ns0:r><ns0:rPr><ns0:rFonts ns0:ascii="Gill Sans Nova" ns0:hAnsi="Gill Sans Nova" /><ns0:szCs ns0:val="20" /><ns0:lang ns0:val="en-US" /></ns0:rPr><ns0:t>’</ns0:t></ns0:r><ns0:r ns0:rsidRPr="008F4FCC"><ns0:rPr><ns0:rFonts ns0:ascii="Gill Sans Nova" ns0:hAnsi="Gill Sans Nova" /><ns0:szCs ns0:val="20" /><ns0:lang ns0:val="en-US" /></ns0:rPr><ns0:t>s name.</ns0:t></ns0:r></ns0:p><ns0:p ns5:paraId="1465415D" ns5:textId="30227961" ns0:rsidR="005F3A45" ns0:rsidRPr="008F4FCC" ns0:rsidRDefault="00386A52" ns0:rsidP="008F4FCC"><ns0:pPr><ns0:spacing ns0:after="0" /><ns0:rPr><ns0:rFonts ns0:ascii="Gill Sans Nova" ns0:hAnsi="Gill Sans Nova" /><ns0:szCs ns0:val="20" /><ns0:lang ns0:val="en-US" /></ns0:rPr></ns0:pPr><ns0:r ns0:rsidRPr="008F4FCC"><ns0:rPr><ns0:rFonts ns0:ascii="Gill Sans Nova" ns0:hAnsi="Gill Sans Nova" /><ns0:szCs ns0:val="20" /><ns0:lang ns0:val="en-US" /></ns0:rPr><ns0:t>It’s important when we meet people to catch their names and use them in conversation.</ns0:t></ns0:r></ns0:p></ns0:txbxContent></ns4:txbx><ns4:bodyPr rot="0" vert="horz" wrap="square" lIns="108000" tIns="108000" rIns="108000" bIns="108000" anchor="t" anchorCtr="0"><ns3:noAutofit /></ns4:bodyPr></ns4:wsp></ns3:graphicData></ns3:graphic><ns2:sizeRelH relativeFrom="margin"><ns2:pctWidth>0</ns2:pctWidth></ns2:sizeRelH><ns2:sizeRelV relativeFrom="margin"><ns2:pctHeight>0</ns2:pctHeight></ns2:sizeRelV></ns1:anchor></ns0:drawing>